CHAPTER 11
Personal Jurisdiction
We turn now from jurisdiction over the plaintiff’s claims to jurisdiction over the parties, especially the defendant and his property. Jurisdiction over the claims in an action is called subject matter jurisdiction and was the subject of the previous chapter. Jurisdiction over the parties is called personal jurisdiction, and this chapter examines personal jurisdiction.

The basic idea behind personal jurisdiction is that a court of a specific state cannot enter a judgment against anyone. There are limits on whose rights any state’s courts can decide. For example, most readers of this book have had no contact with the Maldives. You have probably never set foot in the Maldives, done business with anyone in the Maldives, nor agreed to litigate any cases in the courts of the Maldives. Now imagine that you are summoned to defend a claim against you in a Maldivian court. The Maldives is a beautiful country, popular as a tourist attraction, and has an independent judiciary. Yet the idea of defending yourself in Maldivian court is probably shocking to you. Why?

You might answer that you have no connection to the Maldives, and a country’s courts should not be able to decide the fate of people who have no connection to that country. This is the core principle that animates the doctrines of personal jurisdiction: the courts of a state can adjudicate only the rights of those who have enough of a connection to the state to justify the exercise of the state’s judicial authority over them.

Before we proceed, it is important to emphasize that doctrines of subject matter jurisdiction and of personal jurisdiction are distinct. They are related in the sense that they both deal with jurisdiction—the power of a court to enter a binding judgment in a case. A federal court cannot enter a binding judgment against a defendant if it lacks subject matter jurisdiction over the action. The same is true if the court lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant. But
