subject matter jurisdiction and personal jurisdiction are independent concepts. A court can have subject matter jurisdiction but lack personal jurisdiction, and vice versa. As we shall see, personal jurisdiction places limits on the power of both state courts and federal courts to hear cases, unlike the rules of subject matter jurisdiction we have studied, which limit only the power of the federal courts. And while subject matter jurisdiction is grounded in Article III of the Constitution, we shall see that personal jurisdiction rests on very different foundations.

