Historical Foundations
The notion that a court cannot enter a binding decree unless it has jurisdiction over the defendant is ancient. And long before the Fourteenth Amendment, courts in the United States and elsewhere held that, in most cases, they could not exercise jurisdiction over defendants who were not present within the sovereign territory of their government. A court in Virginia, for example, would generally decline to adjudicate claims against a defendant living in Georgia. But this forbearance by courts was not the product of constitutional compulsion (and indeed, it pre-existed the Constitution). Rather, it was a product of comity—the mutual respect among different sovereigns. The courts of Virginia would not inject themselves into the affairs of Georgia, and vice versa.

This changed after the Civil War, when the Reconstruction Amendments, and in particular the Fourteenth Amendment, placed new constitutional constraints on what the states of the United States could do. As we have seen before, the Fourteenth Amendment prohibits states—including state courts—from depriving anyone of “life, liberty, or property, without due process of law.” Does the constitutional guarantee of due process limit the power of state courts to enter judgments against certain defendants who cannot be found in the same state? Ten years after the ratification of the Fourteenth Amendment, the Supreme Court addressed this question in Pennoyer v. Neff.

Pennoyer v. Neff
_________________________

95 U.S. 714 (1878)

JUSTICE FIELD delivered the opinion of the court.

This is an action to recover the possession of a tract of land, of the alleged value of $15,000, situated in the State of Oregon. The plaintiff [Neff, respondent here,] asserts title to the premises by a patent of the United States issued to him in 1866, under the act of Congress of Sept. 27, 1850, usually known as the Donation Law of Oregon. The defendant [Pennoyer, petitioner here,] claims to have acquired the premises under a sheriff’s deed, made upon a sale of the property on execution issued upon a judgment recovered against the plaintiff in one of the circuit courts of the State. The case turns upon the validity of this judgment.
